# Caddy in front of Fit-Check. Installed to /etc/caddy/Caddyfile by
# deploy/bootstrap.sh, and by `deploy/redeploy.sh cutover` when it differs from the
# installed file (the file it replaces is kept at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.prev).
#
# The public name is written once, as the first site block's address below. That
# line is the one place it lives for the server: Caddy reads no environment variable
# and no .env for it. deploy/redeploy.sh and deploy/check_live.py name it only to
# check it, and a test keeps them in step with this file.
#
# Logs: Caddy's own log and the access log both go to stderr, which systemd puts in
# the journal: `sudo journalctl -u caddy`.
#
# Uploads: each site refuses a request body over 10 MB with a 413 before it reaches
# the app. The app's own checks are tighter (a photo stops at 6 MB, an inventory
# CSV at 1 MB); this is the outer bound on every request.

# Automatic HTTPS: 443 with a Let's Encrypt certificate that Caddy fetches and renews
# itself (HTTP-01 on port 80), and plain HTTP for this name redirected here with a 308.
fitcheck.care {
	log
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
	request_body {
		max_size 10MB
	}
}

# The compatibility door: the bare IP keeps answering on plain HTTP, with no redirect,
# for anyone holding the old URL and for the deploy checks, which must not depend on
# DNS. No page, doc or slide gives this as a URL to open.
http://147.224.201.81 {
	log
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
	request_body {
		max_size 10MB
	}
}
